        <!-- Section: Notation -->
        <section class="article-section" id="section-cbrt-notation">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 17c2 0 2.8-1 2.8-2.8V10c0-2 1-3.3 3.2-3"/><path d="M9 11.2h5.7"/></svg></span> Notation</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">Cube Root Notation: What Does the ∛ Symbol Mean?</h2>
                <p>The symbol <strong>∛</strong> is the cube root sign. It's built from the same radical symbol used for square roots, but with a small "3" (called the index) tucked into the notch.</p>
                <div class="examples-grid">
                    <div class="example-card"><span class="example-expr">∛27</span><span class="example-reason">"the cube root of 27"</span></div>
                    <div class="example-card"><span class="example-expr">27 = radicand</span><span class="example-reason">The number inside the symbol</span></div>
                    <div class="example-card"><span class="example-expr">3 = index</span><span class="example-reason">How many times to multiply</span></div>
                </div>
                <p>You'll also see cube roots written as a fractional exponent:</p>
                <div class="math-block"><span class="math-formula">x^(1/3) = ∛x</span></div>
                <p>Both forms mean the same thing — the exponent version is often used in calculators, spreadsheets, and programming because the ∛ symbol isn't always easy to type.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <figure class="section-image">
            <img src="/images/cbrt-notation.jpg" alt="Simple diagram showing ∛x = y, labeling the index (3), radical sign, radicand, and root" loading="lazy" width="800" height="450">
            <figcaption>The anatomy of a cube root expression and its mathematical terms</figcaption>
