        <!-- Section: Simplification Table -->
        <section class="article-section" id="section-cbrt-simplification">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m12 3-1.9 5.8a2 2 0 0 1-1.3 1.3L3 12l5.8 1.9a2 2 0 0 1 1.3 1.3L12 21l1.9-5.8a2 2 0 0 1 1.3-1.3L21 12l-5.8-1.9a2 2 0 0 1-1.3-1.3z"/></svg></span> Simplification</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">Cube Root Simplification Table</h2>
                <p>Not every cube root is a perfect cube, but many can still be simplified by pulling out any perfect-cube factors.</p>
                <div class="table-scroll-wrapper">
                    <table class="data-table data-table--compact">
                        <thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Simplified Form</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>∛16</td><td>2∛2</td><td>16 = 8 × 2, and ∛8 = 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>∛24</td><td>2∛3</td><td>24 = 8 × 3</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>∛40</td><td>2∛5</td><td>40 = 8 × 5</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>∛54</td><td>3∛2</td><td>54 = 27 × 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>∛81</td><td>3∛3</td><td>81 = 27 × 3</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>∛128</td><td>4∛2</td><td>128 = 64 × 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>∛250</td><td>5∛2</td><td>250 = 125 × 2</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>The trick: find the largest perfect cube that divides evenly into your number, pull it out, and the rest stays under the radical.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

